Ladybird names a key by its position in a KeyboardEvent’s code, and
the Qt UI takes that position from the native key code: the macOS
virtual key, the Windows scan code, or the XKB keycode less 8. Run on
Linux X11 with real key events under setxkbmap us, de, ua and fr,
Chrome 149, Firefox 151 and WebKit 26.5 (GTK) all do the same for code:
the German key at the US Y position gives key “z” and code “KeyY”.
For keyCode, a letter key that types a Cyrillic letter gives 70 for the
F position in Chrome, and in Firefox once an ASCII-capable layout is
configured alongside (ua,us), and 0 in WebKit; Ladybird gives 70. The
AZERTY digit-row key that types “&” gives 49 in Chrome and Firefox and
55 in WebKit; Ladybird gives 49. The German “ö” key gives 192 in
Chrome, 59 in Firefox and 0 in WebKit; Ladybird’s 0 is unchanged.
Chrome’s DomCodeFromNSEvent swaps kVK_ISO_Section and kVK_ANSI_Grave on
an ISO keyboard; Ladybird maps only kVK_ISO_Section, to Backquote, and
leaves kVK_ANSI_Grave to the key the layout reports.

All three engines agree on the keys a layout names: The AZERTY key at
the US M position, which types a comma, gives keyCode 188, and Ctrl
with the German key at the US Y position gives 90; Ladybird does too.
